and so what i'll do in my part of the  presentation i'll start by introducing  the omixlogic program and then i will  pass it on to dr raghav who is going to  be speaking about his  uh  kind of  detailed review of the curriculum and  the types of exercises and projects that  we will conduct  so the omixlogic metagenomics program  is a program designed to introduce the  participants to the role of the microbes  but also to the practical aspects of  data analysis to understand from data  generation to processing to downstream  biological interpretation so what's  important about metagenomics is that  metagenomics is different from many  other types of omics because it is  trying to characterize a community  instead of an individual  so what this program does is it is  organized into several different  sections  we will first cover the logic  why we are trying to ask specific  questions how do we ask specific  questions what are the results that  we're expecting to get then we will talk  about practice how do you practically  try to apply these methods of analysis  and prepare for independence and that  leads us into the project where you can  identify a data set  and plan your analysis and execute it  with the help of technical support  the logic focuses primarily on the  different types of data that  metagenomics covers and the specific  questions that we would be asking using  that data  then the practice leads us into the  specific analytical approaches so that  we understand in detail what are the  types of analyses and what are the  outcomes of those analyses and how do we  know what is significant what is not  significant what is signal and where is  noise  we have to understand that  so in bioinformatics  there's an environment where this kind  of an analysis takes place and that  includes being able to design  sequential  combinations of code which are called  pipelines and so those pipelines provide  us with the outcomes that we can then  try to interpret interpret the  composition of the microbiome in the  environment how does the environmental  or the gut microbiome can be associated  with behavior can we look at the gut  microbiome the skin microbiome can we  identify associations with clinical  factors etc so leading from  the very basic understanding of the  logic  all the way to the application where you  can start analyzing specific data sets  and ask these important questions  the program is organized into several  sessions that are separated by the type  of application the type of project or  the type of analysis that you're going  to be doing  now for those of you that might be  completely new to the area of  metagenomics i want to encourage all of  you to try to complete the free  introduction which is called bytes and  molecules on the portal so sonorica  already introduced you to the portal if  you have an account this is a free  course and you will see there that one  of those is an introduction to  metagenomics so in that course you will  be able to understand the terminology  and the specific types of applications  of metagenomics  what we will do is then later on if you  would like to prepare is you can try to  go through the metagenomics data  analysis course and in that course we  cover data generation types of projects  and data sets and all the way into the  specific analysis and what we will do in  the program is we will actually take a  look at the logic behind the analysis  why these methods were created  how do we apply them what do they  actually produce and we will take a  chance to practice all of these analyses  first of all using our point-and-click  interface called the t-biome platform  a good example that i will demonstrate  here in just a second is the data 2  pipeline so the data 2 pipeline is  divided up into several stages  from pre-processing to then  actual  connecting sort of denoising what's  called denoising data 2 stands for  denoising  and then assignment of taxonomy and  visualization and then we will use this  pipeline to actually understand  different types of visualizations  so let's just briefly take a look  on your portal  on your profile  you will find that with that profile you  should have access to our platform and  this is the link and this platform  combines many of the standard tools that  we typically use for processing and  analysis of data  and if you  follow my example i pasted the link in  the chat you will see that there's a  whole section here dedicated to  metagenomics and that includes three  different sections metagenomics or  microbiome  metagenomics using kind 2 and amplicon  16 has an 18s which looks at  data to and denoising the reeds so that  we can assign taxonomy  so in each one of these sections we have  a demo pipeline and the demo pipeline  actually allows us to understand the  analysis  as we build the pipeline  so going through this interface we're  able to actually see how to connect the  individual methods into a pipeline  and uh  one second  why  this is not loading  okay  let me actually try this again  okay  so after you complete this pipeline you  will also find that there's  visualization that makes it easier to  start looking at the data and  understanding the composition of each  sample comparing the samples in their  proportionate abundance and  understanding the differences between  the different types of  samples that we have using a variety of  statistical and analytical methods so  like here we can see nmds  so in this program and briefly i will uh  just mention several of the important uh  kind of aspects of the microbiome  we will learn about microbiome in the  human body including the skin and the  gut or the stomach  where you will understand the different  types of compositions associated with  different kinds of phenotypes we will  look at how these bacteria form  communities and how we can analyze these  communities to understand the  relationship between  a specific phenotype that we can  interpret in a clinical or a phenotypic  way and the actual composition of the  microbes and finally we will look at  collections of data sets where you can  sample  a specific type of samples  to ask questions  so in conclusion this program is going  to give you the tools  and the logic behind the analysis so  that you can be independent in asking  research questions and conducting the  analysis  getting the results that you can publish  or potentially put into a poster  presentation  so with that i will pass on my screen to  dr raghav
 

